1
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:15,560
Il ruolo della casa è che parliamo in inglese, ma chi vuole parlare in italiano è permesso

2
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:22,520
di farlo, anche perché c'è, non so se c'è interpretazione.

3
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,220
Does this mean that there is interpretation, direttore?

4
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:41,300
Okay, just not to get emotional. Well, today we discuss here the primaries,

5
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:49,760
and the occasion is given to us by the fact that the author of a book on the USA elections

6
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:55,600
and political system, Donatella Campus and Gianfranco Pasquino, are here with us.

7
00:00:56,460 --> 00:01:08,840
So it's a wonderful opportunity for us to dig into one of the most intriguing sort of

8
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:16,920
processes of western democracies, which is the primaries in the US, basically.

9
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:25,540
We Italians look at the primaries with a sort of mixed feeling. We like them, but we fear them.

10
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:33,800
Not the American ones, but the idea of having primaries in this country as well.

11
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:42,820
Gianfranco is a supporter of the primaries. Politician is not a real politician, he was

12
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:52,000
in parliament for a few years, but without really being affected in the sense that he

13
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:03,080
has remained what he was, which I appreciate. Because primaries, on the one side difficult

14
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:11,700
to control, on the other side may be controlled by somebody. And these opposite reasons are

15
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:18,980
enough to make several politicians wary of them. If I'm not sure that I can control them,

16
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,880
why should I want to undergo such a complicated process of selection of candidates?

17
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:38,240
At the same time, they require a sort of cultural tradition that perhaps we lack in this country.

18
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:48,240
We may learn something. At the same time, when we read about the history of the primaries in the US,

19
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:56,380
we also have voices that say that before the primaries it was worse, but perhaps it was also better.

20
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:03,820
And we know everything also about this thing. But whatever we think about the primaries,

21
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:14,520
the fact remains that as we see them from outside, it's a sort of vital process of democracy.

22
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:23,100
This is undeniable. Because the main issues that a country has to decide upon by the following elections

23
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:33,780
come out, are debated. Candidates are forced to take a standing on them, opposing to each other,

24
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:41,420
and you can understand how they really think, whether they are consistent or not,

25
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:50,260
what they said in the past, what they are saying now, etc. And in terms of understanding by the citizens

26
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:59,220
of what is topical for the political arena in that precise moment of history,

27
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:07,180
while it's a wonderful kind of instrument to spread around issues and to understand

28
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:16,440
whether something is more important than something else. Of course, there is an aspect that has to be considered.

29
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:28,480
I suppose that in Malta or in Latvia or in Slovenia they would be much less expensive than they are in a big country.

30
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:41,700
And therefore you have this kind of profile of our democracies in the big countries that has to be somehow reflected upon.

31
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:54,980
How much does it cost to be a candidate first in the primaries and then in the electoral process afterward?

32
00:04:55,540 --> 00:05:05,180
How the money is collected? Where does the money come from? Does it affect the freedom of the candidates

33
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:15,540
and afterwards of the elected officials? So in my view this is also something that we should consider,

34
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:22,860
the cost of democracy in other words. But that's why it's a very mixed process,

35
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:32,960
because it is essential for the citizens to understand, it is essential to have money to allow citizens to understand.

36
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:40,200
And the former aspect is something that strengthens democracy,

37
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,800
the latter aspect might be something that weakens democracy. So I mean it's a very interesting topic to discuss.

38
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:59,900
I've used my time even more than I should. You are one, two, three, four, five. If you remain...

39
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:04,900
...through November, precinct captains to organize each of those 3,000 precincts.

40
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:10,120
And they have 3,000 team leaders who are volunteers that work with those paid precinct captains.

41
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:15,980
So they're paying attention to the fight. They know the same 17 states and the same 3,000 precincts within those states,

42
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:19,620
that one or two percent of the votership that will decide this election.

43
00:06:20,540 --> 00:06:23,800
So it's going to be a very tough election and I would predict it will be a very close election.

44
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:28,140
And as I argued recently in an op-ed in the Washington Post, which some of you may have read,

45
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:31,960
we could very well, and I'll make this my closing point before they throw me out,

46
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:37,060
we could very well have a situation because of our electoral system and its electoral college

47
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:40,220
where you have to win the votes in the series of the provinces of the states.

48
00:06:41,220 --> 00:06:45,300
Where Bush wins again without winning the popular vote and is sent back for four more years

49
00:06:45,300 --> 00:06:47,760
even though he comes in second in the national popular vote.

50
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:53,340
And because the elections are so close, and Claudio and I agree, I think we are on the cusp of what's called a new party system

51
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:59,340
because in such turmoil and imbalance out of that normally comes a party system

52
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:02,340
when it's very divided and our country is very divided and divisive.

53
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:06,540
It could also go the other way. It could be that Kerry loses the popular vote

54
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:11,180
but gets his votes in the right combination of states to win the electoral college

55
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:14,840
and Bush leaves under the same conditions that he arrived.

56
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:21,200
So watch for something interesting, magical, perhaps tragic to happen in November.

57
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:24,760
And on that note, I guess I'm definitely out of time. Thank you.

58
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:27,900
Thank you. It was very exciting, ABB.

59
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:30,560
But this may happen anywhere.

60
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:36,520
Wherever you have a majoritarian system where the vote is highly concentrated,

61
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:41,880
you might have a candidate that scores more votes but fewer seats.

62
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:44,800
So in the UK it has happened frequently.

63
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:49,060
I want to tell you something about fundraising and President Bush.

64
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:55,940
Because when I was in New York days ago I saw a letter that he sent to all the families

65
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:59,460
having a flat in Fifth Avenue.

66
00:07:59,460 --> 00:08:00,880
Let's hope so.

67
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:03,980
It's here, 50 meters deep.

68
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:06,440
Get out before going home.

69
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:08,600
It's better not to find it, it creates expectations.

70
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:12,460
It's a risk, but tomorrow they'll forget it.

71
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:18,480
I think there will be questions because the theme is too exciting.

72
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,460
Ranieri, Ambassador Pallarini.

73
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,660
And the microphone, if you wait a second.

74
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:30,140
Thank you very much.

75
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:35,910
È stata menzionata varie volte da varie autori la specialità americana,

76
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:39,740
la specialità della democrazia americana.

77
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:45,940
Ora, io so che Giuliano Amato è nato come costituzionalista,

78
00:08:46,320 --> 00:08:49,080
quindi ho un po' paura di dire quello che dico ora.

79
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:53,660
Cioè che quello che è speciale, più che l'abito costituzionale,

80
00:08:53,660 --> 00:09:00,040
il sistema, le primaries, gli equilibri fra i vari poteri,

81
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:04,420
perdono disponibilità a svolgere quel ruolo

82
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:07,420
che tecnicamente si chiama socializzazione politica.

83
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,740
Questo è il discorso sul capitale sociale.

84
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:13,500
Cioè se i cittadini non si associano fra di loro

85
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,900
si riduce, si erode un po' il tessuto democratico.

86
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:19,720
Questo è il vero problema americano,

87
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:23,840
perché se poi andiamo a vedere l'associabilità dei francesi è molto bassa,

88
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:27,020
l'associabilità degli italiani è alquanto bassa

89
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,720
e in Europa soltanto in un certo senso i paesi dell'Europa centrosettentrionale

90
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:36,020
hanno un'alta sociabilità, spesso collegata fra l'altro alla pratica religiosa,

91
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:40,760
che come sappiamo negli Stati Uniti continua a essere un elemento molto forte della sociabilità.

92
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,840
L'ultimo punto è una semplice battuta,

93
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:49,220
perché noi nel frattempo abbiamo deciso che non demonizziamo i candidati politici,

94
00:09:49,220 --> 00:09:54,520
ma abbiamo deciso che ABB vuol dire amato before Berlusconi.

